  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\nA `Set-Cookie` header using HTAB (horizontal tab) instead of SPACE before the `Secure` attribute — e.g. `Set-Cookie: sid=SECRET;\\tSecure` — causes curl to store the cookie without its Secure flag. The cookie is then sent over plaintext HTTP on subsequent requests to the same host.\n\nThe space-separated form `Set-Cookie: sid=SECRET; Secure` works correctly.\n\n## Affected versions\n\ncurl 8.13.0 through at least 8.21.0 (all versions containing commit `1aea05a6c26`, \"cookie: convert to using strparse\", 2025-02-18).\n\n## Root cause\n\nIn `lib/cookie.c`, `parse_cookie_header()` uses a do/while loop that parses each cookie attribute:\n\n```c\ndo {\n    if(!curlx_str_cspn(&ptr, &name, \";\\t\\r\\n=\")) {\n        // parse attribute\n    }\n} while(!curlx_str_single(&ptr, ';'));\n```\n\nAfter consuming the `;` separator, `ptr` points at the HTAB. Since `\\t` is in the reject set of `curlx_str_cspn`, the function sees it as the first character, returns an empty match (failure), and the `if` block is skipped. Then `curlx_str_single(&ptr, ';')` fails because the current byte is `\\t`, not `;`, so the loop exits. The `Secure` attribute is never parsed, but the cookie is still stored.\n\nThe previous parser (before `1aea05a6c26`) had `while(ISBLANK(*ptr)) ptr++;` before parsing each attribute, which correctly skipped HTAB. This blank-skipping was lost in the strparse conversion. The helper `curlx_str_passblanks()` exists in the codebase but is not called here.\n\n## Reproduction\n1. Build curl at commit c2676bf9e639e31e44b12a2cc82cf2046231b056 (or any version from 8.13.0 onward) with OpenSSL support enabled.\n\n2. Generate a self-signed TLS certificate for the hostname test.invalid, valid for at least one day.\n\n3. Start a local server that listens on two ports:\n\nHTTPS on port 8443 — serves Set-Cookie: sid=HTAB_SECRET;\\tSecure (with a literal HTAB character 0x09 before Secure) when the path is /htab, and Set-Cookie: sid=SPACE_SECRET; Secure (with a normal space) when the path is /space.\nHTTP on port 8080 — echoes back the received Cookie header value in the response body.\n\n4. Set the HTAB cookie over HTTPS by requesting https://test.invalid:8443/htab, saving the cookie jar:\n```\ncurl -ks --noproxy '*' \\\n  --resolve test.invalid:8443:127.0.0.1 \\\n  -c /tmp/htab.jar \\\n  https://test.invalid:8443/htab\nMake a follow-up request over plaintext HTTP using the saved cookie jar:\n```\n```\ncurl -sS --noproxy '*' \\\n  --resolve test.invalid:8080:127.0.0.1 \\\n  -b /tmp/htab.jar \\\n  http://test.invalid:8080/\n```\n6. Observe that the cookie is sent over HTTP — the response body will contain sid=HTAB_SECRET, proving the Secure flag was silently dropped and the cookie leaked over plaintext.\n\n7. Inspect the cookie jar — the fourth field (Secure flag) for the HTAB cookie will be FALSE, confirming curl did not store the Secure attribute.\n\n8. Run the control case — repeat steps 4-5 using the /space endpoint instead, which sets the cookie with a normal space before Secure. The follow-up HTTP request will receive <none> — the cookie is correctly withheld. The cookie jar will show the Secure flag as TRUE.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nAdd `curlx_str_passblanks(&ptr);` after the `;` is consumed in the do/while loop, before `curlx_str_cspn` parses the next attribute name. Add a regression test comparing `;\\tSecure` against `; Secure` and verifying neither cookie is returned for an HTTP URL.\n\n## Relationship to existing work\n\nThis is distinct from PR #21185 / test 1685 (commit `8e8bdd3604`), which addresses HTAB *inside* a cookie attribute value (e.g. `Path=/foo\\tbar`). This issue concerns HTAB *before* an attribute name, causing the attribute to be silently discarded.\n\n## Impact\n\n## Summary:\n\nThe `Secure` flag is a confidentiality boundary that prevents cookies from being sent over unencrypted connections. Silently dropping it means a session cookie intended to be HTTPS-only is exposed on any subsequent HTTP request to the same host. A network attacker observing that HTTP traffic can capture the cookie. All attributes at or after the HTAB position are similarly skipped (e.g. `HttpOnly`).\n\nHTAB is valid optional whitespace per both RFC 6265 section 5.2 and the HTTP grammar (OWS). The old parser handled it correctly.",
